# Applicable Joint Controller Relationships Scope
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> The upstream — which multi-party PII relationships involve joint control (as opposed to controller-processor or independent controllers). Many orgs have zero joint arrangements.
## What this template gives you
This template helps you clearly identify and document any situations where your organization shares responsibility for personal data with another party, as opposed to acting alone or as a processor.
## When to use it
Use this document whenever you need to determine if you have any joint controller relationships, especially when your business activities or partnerships change. Update it as needed when new relationships are formed or existing ones change.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 30 to 45 minutes completing this from scratch, as you''ll need to consider and describe up to three required elements.
## 1. Joint-control test — jointly determine purposes AND means (both prongs required)
<<MUST item:A.7.2.7:scope_joint_test>>
_Why: GDPR Art.4.7 + Art.26.1_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 2. In-scope arrangements enumerated (or ''none'' with rationale)
<<MUST item:A.7.2.7:scope_arrangements_list>>
_Why: Coverage_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 3. Relationships excluded (controller-processor → A.7.2.6 / independent-controllers → contract-not-arrangement)
<<MUST item:A.7.2.7:scope_exclusions>>
_Why: Classification defensibility_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Recommended additions
_The items below strengthen the artefact but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Fill in any that apply to your environment._
### 1. Trigger list (new partnership / new co-branded service)
<<SHOULD item:A.7.2.7:scope_change_drivers>>
_Why: Currency_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__A_7_2_7__applicable_scope.md', 3, 1, '2026-08-09 12:24:34.699322+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.templates (leaf_id, template_version, body_md, source_file, must_count, should_count, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('req:A.7.2.7:joint_controller_procedure', 1, '---
leaf_id: req:A.7.2.7:joint_controller_procedure
control_ref: A.7.2.7
standard_id: ISO27701:2019
evidence_type: procedure
trigger_type: profile_fact
template_version: 1
must_count: 6
should_count: 1
# Joint PII Controller Arrangement Procedure
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> §7.2.7 requires a documented arrangement with every joint controller — respective roles + responsibilities + subject-facing single point of contact. Bridges to GDPR Art.26 (arrangement + essence available to subjects + Art.26.3 subject can exercise rights against either party).
## What this template gives you
This template helps you document how you and another organization jointly manage personal data, clearly outlining each party’s responsibilities and providing a single contact point for individuals.
## When to use it
Use this procedure whenever you share control of personal data with another organization, and update it whenever your joint arrangement changes or as needed to stay compliant.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 60 to 90 minutes completing this from scratch, as each required section takes around 10 to 15 minutes to fill in thoughtfully.
## 1. Classification test — when a relationship is joint control (jointly determine purposes + means) vs controller-processor vs independent controllers
<<MUST item:A.7.2.7:proc_classification_test>>
_Why: GDPR Art.4.7 + Art.26.1_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 2. Documented arrangement form — contract or equivalent binding document
<<MUST item:A.7.2.7:proc_arrangement_form>>
_Why: §7.2.7 — contract or similar binding document_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 3. Role + responsibility allocation (security / notice / rights fulfilment / breach notification / DPIA)
<<MUST item:A.7.2.7:proc_role_allocation>>
_Why: §7.2.7 implementation guidance_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 4. Essence-of-arrangement publication — subject-facing summary of who does what, per Art.26.2
<<MUST item:A.7.2.7:proc_essence_publication>>
_Why: GDPR Art.26.2_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 5. Single point of contact + subject-rights routing (subjects can exercise against either controller regardless of arrangement)
<<MUST item:A.7.2.7:proc_rights_exercise>>
_Why: GDPR Art.26.3_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 6. Breach-notification coordination — who notifies SA + subjects
<<MUST item:A.7.2.7:proc_breach_coordination>>
_Why: Art.33 + Art.34 joint handling_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Recommended additions
_The items below strengthen the artefact but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Fill in any that apply to your environment._
### 1. Dispute-resolution mechanism between joint controllers
<<SHOULD item:A.7.2.7:proc_dispute_resolution>>
_Why: Operational resilience_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__A_7_2_7__joint_controller_procedure.md', 6, 1, '2026-08-09 12:24:34.699322+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
