# DPO Tasks Procedure
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> Art.39 enumerates the DPO''s tasks (Art.39.1 a-e + Art.39.2 risk-based approach). The procedure documents how each task is operationalised in practice
## What this template gives you
This template helps you clearly document how your Data Protection Officer (DPO) carries out their key responsibilities under GDPR, making it easier to show compliance during audits or reviews.
## When to use it
Use this procedure whenever your organization is required to appoint a DPO, or when your data processing activities match the relevant GDPR criteria. Update it whenever there are changes to your DPO''s tasks or processes.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 60 to 75 minutes completing this template from scratch, as each required section will take roughly 10 to 15 minutes to fill in with your specific practices.
## 1. Inform + advise the controller/processor + employees who carry out processing (Art.39.1.a)
<<MUST item:Art.39:inform_advise>>
_Why: Art.39.1.a_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 2. Monitor compliance with GDPR + MS data protection provisions + internal policies (Art.39.1.b)
<<MUST item:Art.39:monitor_compliance>>
_Why: Art.39.1.b_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 3. Provide DPIA advice + monitor performance per Art.39.1.c (links to Art.35)
<<MUST item:Art.39:dpia_advice>>
_Why: Art.39.1.c_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 4. Cooperate with SA — point of contact for prior consultation (Art.39.1.d-e)
<<MUST item:Art.39:sa_cooperation>>
_Why: Art.39.1.d-e_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 5. Risk-based approach (Art.39.2 — having due regard to the risk associated with processing)
<<MUST item:Art.39:risk_based>>
_Why: Art.39.2_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Recommended additions
_The items below strengthen the artefact but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Fill in any that apply to your environment._
