should_count: 1
# Applicable Consent Activities Scope
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> The upstream that bounds the register — which processing activities rely on consent (per Art.6.1.a) vs other lawful bases. Without this, consent gets over-applied (worst case: ''consent default'') or under-applied (worst case: covert reliance)
## What this template gives you
This template helps you clearly define which of your data processing activities require consent under GDPR, and which rely on other legal grounds. It prevents confusion or mistakes about when to ask for consent.
## When to use it
Use this whenever you are reviewing or setting up your data processing activities, as it always applies to your environment. Update it as needed whenever your processing activities or legal bases change.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 30 to 45 minutes completing this from scratch, as each required section takes around 10-15 minutes to fill in thoughtfully.
## 1. Activities using consent as the lawful basis enumerated (drawn from the Art.6 lawful basis register)
<<MUST item:Art.7:scope_consent_activities>>
_Why: Art.6.1.a_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 2. Rules for when consent overlaps with another basis (Art.7 still applies if consent is the chosen basis even when another basis would also work)
<<MUST item:Art.7:scope_overlap_rules>>
_Why: Defensible bounding_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 3. Special-category overlay rule — Art.9.2.a explicit consent has stricter capture requirements than Art.6.1.a
<<MUST item:Art.7:scope_special_overlay>>
_Why: Art.9.2.a_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Recommended additions
_The items below strengthen the artefact but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Fill in any that apply to your environment._
### 1. Trigger list for re-scoping (new feature requiring consent, basis change for an existing activity)
<<SHOULD item:Art.7:scope_change_drivers>>
_Why: Currency_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__Art_7__applicable_activities_scope.md', 3, 1, '2026-08-09 12:24:34.699322+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.templates (leaf_id, template_version, body_md, source_file, must_count, should_count, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('req:Art.7:consent_management_procedure', 1, '---
leaf_id: req:Art.7:consent_management_procedure
control_ref: Art.7
standard_id: GDPR:2016/679
evidence_type: procedure
trigger_type: universal
template_version: 1
must_count: 6
should_count: 1
# Consent Management Procedure
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> Art.7 requires the controller to demonstrate consent was given, in a clearly distinguishable form, with the right to withdraw being as easy as giving. The procedure is the canonical artefact — capture mechanism, withdrawal mechanism, evidence retention. Sibling leaves: consent register, applicable activities scope, program review
## What this template gives you
This template helps you document how your organization collects, manages, and records consent from individuals, ensuring you meet GDPR requirements for clear consent and easy withdrawal.
## When to use it
Use this procedure whenever your organization collects or manages personal data based on consent, and update it whenever your consent processes change or new activities are added.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 60 to 90 minutes completing this from scratch, depending on the complexity of your consent mechanisms and the number of activities involved.
## 1. Capture mechanism documented (granular, freely given, unambiguous affirmative action — no pre-ticked boxes)
<<MUST item:Art.7:capture_mechanism>>
_Why: Art.7.1 + Art.4(11)_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 2. Consent request distinguishable from other matters (separate, clear-language statement)
<<MUST item:Art.7:distinguishable>>
_Why: Art.7.2_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 3. Withdrawal mechanism documented — must be ''as easy as'' giving consent
<<MUST item:Art.7:withdrawal_mechanism>>
_Why: Art.7.3_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 4. Demonstrability — capture record retained (timestamp, mechanism, scope) per consent event
<<MUST item:Art.7:demonstrability>>
_Why: Art.7.1 — demonstrate_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 5. No conditionality of service on non-essential consent (consent must be ''freely given'')
<<MUST item:Art.7:no_conditionality>>
_Why: Art.7.4_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 6. Named owner of the procedure (DPO or Privacy Lead)
<<MUST item:Art.7:owner>>
_Why: Accountability_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Recommended additions
_The items below strengthen the artefact but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Fill in any that apply to your environment._
### 1. Reference to Art.8 child overlay where service is information-society and may attract minors
<<SHOULD item:Art.7:child_overlay>>
_Why: Cross-article coherence_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__Art_7__consent_management_procedure.md', 6, 1, '2026-08-09 12:24:34.699322+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.templates (leaf_id, template_version, body_md, source_file, must_count, should_count, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('req:Art.85:applicable_activities_scope', 1, '---
