leaf_id: req:A.7.2.5:pia_procedure
control_ref: A.7.2.5
standard_id: ISO27701:2019
evidence_type: procedure
trigger_type: profile_fact
template_version: 1
must_count: 6
should_count: 1
# Privacy Impact Assessment Procedure
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> §7.2.5 requires a documented PIA/DPIA process — when to trigger a PIA, what the assessment covers, who signs off, when to consult the supervisory authority. Bridges to GDPR Art.35 (mandatory-DPIA triggers) + Art.36 (prior consultation).
## What this template gives you
This template helps you document your process for carrying out Privacy Impact Assessments, including when to start one, what to include, who approves it, and when to consult authorities. It''s designed to help you meet privacy requirements under ISO 27701 and GDPR.
## When to use it
Use this template whenever your activities meet specific privacy risk triggers that require a formal assessment, such as processing sensitive data or large-scale profiling. Update it as needed whenever your processes or risks change.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 60 to 90 minutes completing this template from scratch, as each required section takes roughly 10 to 15 minutes to fill out thoughtfully.
## 1. Trigger criteria — new processing / material changes / high-risk indicators (Art.35.3 mandatory list: systematic evaluation + large-scale special-category + systematic monitoring of public spaces + others per SA lists)
<<MUST item:A.7.2.5:proc_trigger_criteria>>
_Why: §7.2.5 — need for PIA + Art.35.3_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 2. Assessment scope — PII types + storage + transfers + retention + risks to subjects + safeguards + residual risk
<<MUST item:A.7.2.5:proc_assessment_scope>>
_Why: §7.2.5 implementation guidance + Art.35.7_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 3. Stakeholder consultation — DPO opinion (Art.35.2) + subject-representative views where appropriate (Art.35.9)
<<MUST item:A.7.2.5:proc_consultation>>
_Why: GDPR Art.35.2 + Art.35.9_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 4. Signoff — DPO signoff + business-owner signoff before processing commences
<<MUST item:A.7.2.5:proc_signoff>>
_Why: §7.2.5 accountability_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 5. Prior-consultation trigger — where residual high risk remains, SA consulted per Art.36
<<MUST item:A.7.2.5:proc_prior_consultation>>
_Why: GDPR Art.36.1_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 6. Alignment with ISO/IEC 29134 methodology (referenced by §7.2.5)
<<MUST item:A.7.2.5:proc_29134_alignment>>
_Why: §7.2.5 — Guidance on privacy impact assessments_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Recommended additions
_The items below strengthen the artefact but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Fill in any that apply to your environment._
### 1. PIA toolkit / template referenced (form + risk-scoring rubric)
<<SHOULD item:A.7.2.5:proc_ref_toolkit>>
_Why: Consistency_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__A_7_2_5__pia_procedure.md', 6, 1, '2026-08-09 12:24:34.699322+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.templates (leaf_id, template_version, body_md, source_file, must_count, should_count, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('req:A.7.2.6:processor_contract_procedure', 1, '---
leaf_id: req:A.7.2.6:processor_contract_procedure
control_ref: A.7.2.6
standard_id: ISO27701:2019
evidence_type: procedure
trigger_type: profile_fact
template_version: 1
must_count: 8
should_count: 1
# PII Processor Contracting Procedure
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> §7.2.6 requires a written contract with every PII processor, addressing implementation of Annex B controls (or their equivalent). Bridges to GDPR Art.28.3 mandatory contract terms (documented instructions / confidentiality / security / subprocessor consent / Art.28.3.h assistance / return-or-delete / audit rights).
## What this template gives you
This template helps you create a clear, compliant procedure for contracting with any company that processes personal data on your behalf, ensuring all key privacy and security requirements are addressed in your agreements.
## When to use it
Use this whenever you engage a new service provider or partner to handle personal data, or when reviewing existing contracts to ensure they meet current privacy standards. Update the procedure as needed when your processing activities or legal requirements change.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 1.5 to 2 hours drafting this procedure from scratch, as it covers eight required elements and one recommended section.
## 1. Written form required — no verbal or informal processor arrangements
<<MUST item:A.7.2.6:proc_written_form>>
_Why: §7.2.6 — written contract_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 2. Art.28.3 terms coverage (subject matter + duration + nature + purpose + PII categories + subject categories + controller/processor obligations)
<<MUST item:A.7.2.6:proc_art28_3_terms>>
_Why: GDPR Art.28.3_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 3. Annex B implementation scope — which B.8.x controls apply per processor engagement (default: all)
<<MUST item:A.7.2.6:proc_annex_b_scope>>
_Why: §7.2.6 — implementation of appropriate controls in Annex B_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 4. Documented-instructions clause (Art.28.3.a) — processor acts only on written controller instructions
<<MUST item:A.7.2.6:proc_documented_instructions>>
_Why: GDPR Art.28.3.a_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 5. Subprocessor consent gate (Art.28.2 + Art.28.4) — general or specific written authorisation
<<MUST item:A.7.2.6:proc_subprocessor_gate>>
_Why: GDPR Art.28.2_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 6. End-of-service disposition (Art.28.3.g) — return or delete all PII with certification
<<MUST item:A.7.2.6:proc_return_or_delete>>
_Why: GDPR Art.28.3.g_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 7. Audit rights (Art.28.3.h) — processor makes available all information + contributes to audits
<<MUST item:A.7.2.6:proc_audit_rights>>
_Why: GDPR Art.28.3.h_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 8. Justification required if any Annex B control is excluded from contract (§5.4.1.3)
<<MUST item:A.7.2.6:proc_exclusion_justification>>
_Why: §7.2.6 — justify exclusion_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Recommended additions
_The items below strengthen the artefact but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Fill in any that apply to your environment._
### 1. Legal counsel review before signature
<<SHOULD item:A.7.2.6:proc_legal_review>>
_Why: Defensibility_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__A_7_2_6__processor_contract_procedure.md', 8, 1, '2026-08-09 12:24:34.699322+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.templates (leaf_id, template_version, body_md, source_file, must_count, should_count, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('req:A.7.2.8:pii_processing_ropa', 1, '---
leaf_id: req:A.7.2.8:pii_processing_ropa
control_ref: A.7.2.8
standard_id: ISO27701:2019
evidence_type: register
trigger_type: profile_fact
freshness_days: 365
template_version: 1
must_count: 8
