INSERT INTO public.templates (leaf_id, template_version, body_md, source_file, must_count, should_count, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('req:Art.25:dpbd_procedure', 1, '---
leaf_id: req:Art.25:dpbd_procedure
control_ref: Art.25
standard_id: GDPR:2016/679
evidence_type: procedure
trigger_type: universal
template_version: 1
must_count: 5
should_count: 1
# Data Protection by Design Procedure
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> Art.25.1 requires the controller to implement T&O measures at the time of determining means of processing AND at the time of processing itself. The procedure governs HOW DPbD reviews are integrated into product/system design — design-stage privacy review checkpoints, decision authority, output (defaults + measures register)
## What this template gives you
This template helps you document how privacy and data protection are considered and built into your products or systems from the very beginning, ensuring you meet GDPR requirements for Data Protection by Design.
## When to use it
Use this procedure whenever you are designing or updating a product or system that processes personal data, and review it whenever there are significant changes to your processing activities or privacy risks.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 60 to 90 minutes completing this template from scratch, depending on the complexity of your systems and the number of privacy measures you need to document.
## 1. Design-stage checkpoint stated (when DPbD review happens in the product / system development lifecycle)
<<MUST item:Art.25:proc_checkpoint>>
_Why: Art.25.1 — at the time of determining means_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 2. Inputs to DPbD review (purpose, data minimisation alternatives, special-category overlay, retention rules)
<<MUST item:Art.25:proc_inputs>>
_Why: Art.25.1 — taking into account_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 3. Decision authority for DPbD outcomes (DPO + product lead + engineering lead)
<<MUST item:Art.25:proc_authority>>
_Why: Defensibility_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 4. Integration with Art.35 DPIA — when DPbD review escalates to DPIA
<<MUST item:Art.25:proc_dpia_integration>>
_Why: Art.35.3.b cross-reference_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 5. Per-review record retained (which design, which decisions, which measures)
<<MUST item:Art.25:proc_record>>
_Why: Art.25.1 — accountability_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Recommended additions
_The items below strengthen the artefact but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Fill in any that apply to your environment._
### 1. Approved-certification reliance (Art.25.3) where the org uses a certification mechanism
<<SHOULD item:Art.25:proc_certification>>
_Why: Art.25.3_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__Art_25__dpbd_procedure.md', 5, 1, '2026-08-09 12:24:34.699322+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.templates (leaf_id, template_version, body_md, source_file, must_count, should_count, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('req:Art.33:notification_procedure', 1, '---
leaf_id: req:Art.33:notification_procedure
control_ref: Art.33
standard_id: GDPR:2016/679
evidence_type: procedure
trigger_type: universal
template_version: 1
must_count: 7
should_count: 1
# Art.33 Breach Notification Procedure
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> The procedure governing how Art.33 notifications get drafted, approved, and dispatched within 72 hours. The notification record is the per-event output; this is the operating discipline that makes 72h achievable
## What this template gives you
This template provides a clear, step-by-step procedure for drafting, approving, and sending GDPR Article 33 breach notifications within the required 72-hour window. It helps ensure your team can respond quickly and consistently to data breaches.
## When to use it
Use this document whenever a data breach occurs in your environment and you need to notify authorities under GDPR. Review and update the procedure as needed to keep it current with your practices.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 70 to 105 minutes completing this template from scratch, as each of the seven required sections will take roughly 10 to 15 minutes to fill out thoughtfully.
## 1. Definition of ''awareness'' (when the 72h clock starts) — typically when the controller has reasonable degree of certainty that a security event has occurred and resulted in a personal data breach (EDPB Guidelines 9/2022 reference)
<<MUST item:Art.33:proc_awareness_definition>>
_Why: Art.33.1 — awareness gate_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 2. Severity gate — when notification is NOT required (Art.33.1 unlikely-to-result-in-risk exception). Decision criteria documented
<<MUST item:Art.33:proc_severity_gate>>
_Why: Art.33.1 — risk exception_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 3. Internal intake — how an operational security event reaches the breach-notification team (links to A.5.25/A.5.26 incident triage)
<<MUST item:Art.33:proc_intake>>
_Why: Cross-control coherence_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 4. Drafting workflow — required-fields template per Art.33.3, DPO/legal sign-off, contact-point
<<MUST item:Art.33:proc_drafting>>
_Why: Art.33.3_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 5. Dispatch path to lead supervisory authority (Art.56 + Art.55 — which SA, which channel, with confirmation of receipt)
<<MUST item:Art.33:proc_dispatch>>
_Why: Art.33.1_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 6. Delay handling — if past 72h, reason explanation in notification (Art.33.1 last sentence)
<<MUST item:Art.33:proc_delay_handling>>
_Why: Art.33.1 — delay reason_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 7. Processor inbound — handling Art.33.2 notifications from sub-processors / processors WE rely on
<<MUST item:Art.33:proc_processor_inbound>>
_Why: Art.33.2_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Recommended additions
_The items below strengthen the artefact but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Fill in any that apply to your environment._
### 1. Named owner of the procedure (DPO + legal counsel)
<<SHOULD item:Art.33:proc_owner>>
_Why: Accountability_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__Art_33__notification_procedure.md', 7, 1, '2026-08-09 12:24:34.699322+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.templates (leaf_id, template_version, body_md, source_file, must_count, should_count, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('req:B.8.5.5:disclosure_decision_procedure', 1, '---
leaf_id: req:B.8.5.5:disclosure_decision_procedure
