# Joint Controller Arrangement
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> Art.26 requires joint controllers to determine in a transparent manner their respective responsibilities. The arrangement is the canonical contractual artefact — distinguishes joint controllers from independent controllers (Art.4(7)) or controller-processor relationships (Art.28)
## What this template gives you
This template helps you clearly outline and agree on responsibilities when you share control of personal data with another organization. It ensures everyone knows their role and meets legal requirements for joint data controllers.
## When to use it
Use this document whenever you and another party jointly decide how and why personal data is processed, and update it whenever your arrangement or responsibilities change.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 60 to 75 minutes to complete this template from scratch, as each required section takes around 10 to 15 minutes to fill in thoughtfully.
## 1. Respective responsibilities for compliance allocated (Art.13/14 information, Art.15-22 rights, Art.32 security, Art.33-34 breach)
<<MUST item:Art.26:respective_responsibilities>>
_Why: Art.26.1_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 2. Essence of the arrangement made available to data subjects (in privacy notice or similar)
<<MUST item:Art.26:relationship_essence>>
_Why: Art.26.2_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 3. Contact point for data subjects identified
<<MUST item:Art.26:contact_point>>
_Why: Art.26.1_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 4. Statement that subject may exercise rights against either controller (Art.26.3)
<<MUST item:Art.26:data_subject_against_either>>
_Why: Art.26.3_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## 5. Signed by both joint controllers with date
<<MUST item:Art.26:signature>>
_Why: Authenticity_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Recommended additions
_The items below strengthen the artefact but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Fill in any that apply to your environment._
### 1. Dispute-resolution mechanism between joint controllers
<<SHOULD item:Art.26:dispute_resolution>>
_Why: Operational discipline_
<<GUIDANCE>>
<<TEXT>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__Art_26__joint_controller_arrangement.md', 5, 1, '2026-08-09 12:24:34.699322+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
