<<MUST item:Art.7:reg_scope>>
_Why: Art.7.1 — granular_
> _Standard text:_ Per-row scope of consent (which activity / purpose)
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Reg Timestamp
<<MUST item:Art.7:reg_timestamp>>
_Why: Currency_
> _Standard text:_ Per-row capture timestamp
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Reg Mechanism
<<MUST item:Art.7:reg_mechanism>>
_Why: Auditability_
> _Standard text:_ Per-row capture mechanism (checkbox UI version, sign-up event id, etc.)
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Reg Withdrawal Status
<<MUST item:Art.7:reg_withdrawal_status>>
_Why: Art.7.3_
> _Standard text:_ Per-row withdrawal status (current / withdrawn-on-date)
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Reg Basis Link
<<MUST item:Art.7:reg_basis_link>>
_Why: Cross-article coherence_
> _Standard text:_ Per-row link to Art.6 lawful basis register entry (Art.6.1.a row)
<<GUIDANCE>>
## Recommended additional columns
_These columns strengthen the register but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Add them to the table if they apply to your environment._
### Reg Consent Version
<<SHOULD item:Art.7:reg_consent_version>>
_Why: Audit defensibility_
> _Standard text:_ Per-row consent-text version captured at time of capture (proves what they agreed to)
<<GUIDANCE>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__Art_7__consent_register.md', 6, 1, '2026-08-09 12:03:26.189546+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.templates (leaf_id, template_version, body_md, source_file, must_count, should_count, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('req:Art.9:special_category_program_review', 1, '---
leaf_id: req:Art.9:special_category_program_review
control_ref: Art.9
standard_id: GDPR:2016/679
evidence_type: review_record
trigger_type: profile_fact
freshness_days: 365
template_version: 1
must_count: 5
should_count: 1
table_shape: true
# Special Category Program Review
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> Annual verification that every Art.9 processing activity has a current Art.9.2 condition justification, safeguards are in place, no quiet onboarding happened (freshness=365)
<!-- TABLE-COLUMNS leaf:req:Art.9:special_category_program_review -->
<!-- column: item:Art.9:rev_date -->
<!-- column: item:Art.9:rev_reviewer -->
<!-- column: item:Art.9:rev_register_currency -->
<!-- column: item:Art.9:rev_safeguards_audit -->
<!-- column: item:Art.9:rev_silent_onboarding -->
<!-- /TABLE-COLUMNS -->
## What this template gives you
This template helps you keep track of all your processing activities involving special category data, making sure each one has a valid legal basis and the right safeguards in place.
## When to use it
Use this template once a year if your organization handles special category data under GDPR, especially when your activities match certain risk or profile triggers.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 1 to 2 hours completing this from scratch, depending on the number of activities you need to review and document.
## Register
Fill one row per record. Each column maps to a MUST item the auditor will check — empty columns count as unsatisfied. Add as many rows as you need.
<!-- EDIT-ZONE-START leaf:req:Art.9:special_category_program_review -->
| Rev Date | Rev Reviewer | Rev Register Currency | Rev Safeguards Audit | Rev Silent Onboarding |
|---|---|---|---|---|
|          |          |          |          |          |
|          |          |          |          |          |
|          |          |          |          |          |
<!-- EDIT-ZONE-END leaf:req:Art.9:special_category_program_review -->
## Column guidance — what to fill in
### Rev Date
<<MUST item:Art.9:rev_date>>
_Why: Periodic accountability_
> _Standard text:_ Review date within the planned interval
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Rev Reviewer
<<MUST item:Art.9:rev_reviewer>>
_Why: Accountability_
> _Standard text:_ Reviewer identity (DPO + executive sponsor)
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Rev Register Currency
<<MUST item:Art.9:rev_register_currency>>
_Why: Cross-leaf coherence_
> _Standard text:_ Register currency check — every active activity has its Art.9.2 condition still appropriate
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Rev Safeguards Audit
<<MUST item:Art.9:rev_safeguards_audit>>
_Why: Art.9.3_
> _Standard text:_ Safeguards audit — Art.9.3 secrecy obligations being enforced (where Art.9.2.h-i applies)
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Rev Silent Onboarding
<<MUST item:Art.9:rev_silent_onboarding>>
_Why: Art.9.1 — prohibition default_
> _Standard text:_ Silent-onboarding sweep — verify no new special-category data ingested without the procedure being invoked
<<GUIDANCE>>
## Recommended additional columns
_These columns strengthen the register but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Add them to the table if they apply to your environment._
### Rev Next Date
<<SHOULD item:Art.9:rev_next_date>>
_Why: Planning_
> _Standard text:_ Next planned review date stated
<<GUIDANCE>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__Art_9__special_category_program_review.md', 5, 1, '2026-08-09 12:03:26.189546+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.templates (leaf_id, template_version, body_md, source_file, must_count, should_count, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('req:B.8.5.8:subcontractor_change_register', 1, '---
leaf_id: req:B.8.5.8:subcontractor_change_register
control_ref: B.8.5.8
standard_id: ISO27701:2019
evidence_type: register
trigger_type: profile_fact
freshness_days: 365
template_version: 1
must_count: 5
should_count: 1
table_shape: true
# Subcontractor Change Register
<<DOC_CONTROL>>
> Per-change-event row — every subcontractor add / replace with customer notification date + objection status. Annual refresh (freshness=365).
<!-- TABLE-COLUMNS leaf:req:B.8.5.8:subcontractor_change_register -->
<!-- column: item:B.8.5.8:reg_change_id -->
<!-- column: item:B.8.5.8:reg_change_type -->
<!-- column: item:B.8.5.8:reg_notification_date -->
<!-- column: item:B.8.5.8:reg_effective_date -->
<!-- column: item:B.8.5.8:reg_objection_status -->
<!-- /TABLE-COLUMNS -->
## What this template gives you
This template helps you keep a clear, up-to-date record of every time you add or replace a subcontractor, including when you notified your customer and whether they raised any objections.
## When to use it
Use this register whenever you change your subcontractors and need to track customer notifications and responses. Plan to review and update it about once a year to keep it current.
## Prerequisites
<<PREREQUISITES>>
## Cross-references
<<CROSS_REFERENCES>>
## Estimated effort
Expect to spend about 10-15 minutes per required detail for each change event. Completing the register from scratch may take around an hour, depending on the number of subcontractor changes you need to record.
## Register
Fill one row per record. Each column maps to a MUST item the auditor will check — empty columns count as unsatisfied. Add as many rows as you need.
<!-- EDIT-ZONE-START leaf:req:B.8.5.8:subcontractor_change_register -->
| Reg Change Id | Reg Change Type | Reg Notification Date | Reg Effective Date | Reg Objection Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|
|          |          |          |          |          |
|          |          |          |          |          |
|          |          |          |          |          |
<!-- EDIT-ZONE-END leaf:req:B.8.5.8:subcontractor_change_register -->
## Column guidance — what to fill in
### Reg Change Id
<<MUST item:B.8.5.8:reg_change_id>>
_Why: Audit trail_
> _Standard text:_ Unique change identifier per row
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Reg Change Type
<<MUST item:B.8.5.8:reg_change_type>>
_Why: §8.5.8_
> _Standard text:_ Change type per row (add / replace / remove)
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Reg Notification Date
<<MUST item:B.8.5.8:reg_notification_date>>
_Why: §8.5.8 — inform customer_
> _Standard text:_ Customer notification date per row
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Reg Effective Date
<<MUST item:B.8.5.8:reg_effective_date>>
_Why: Traceability_
> _Standard text:_ Change effective date per row
<<GUIDANCE>>
### Reg Objection Status
<<MUST item:B.8.5.8:reg_objection_status>>
_Why: §8.5.8 — opportunity to object_
> _Standard text:_ Objection status per row (none / raised / resolved / termination)
<<GUIDANCE>>
## Recommended additional columns
_These columns strengthen the register but are not strictly required for the MUST checks. Add them to the table if they apply to your environment._
### Reg Resolution
<<SHOULD item:B.8.5.8:reg_resolution>>
_Why: Audit trail_
> _Standard text:_ Resolution per row where objection raised
<<GUIDANCE>>
## Revision history
<<REVISION_HISTORY>>
', 'req__B_8_5_8__subcontractor_change_register.md', 5, 1, '2026-08-09 12:03:26.189546+00', 'load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('access_rights_lifecycle', 'Access Rights Lifecycle', 'Identity + credential + access-rights management as an integrated
workflow — A.5.15 (access control policy), A.5.16 (identity),
A.5.17 (authentication information), A.5.18 (access rights).
Covers the joiner-mover-leaver traffic that drives most audit
findings on identity governance.', 'ISO27001:2022', 'Auditors sample identities and trace: creation (A.5.16), initial
credential issuance (A.5.17), access grants (A.5.18) — then look
for revocation on role change or leaver events. The bidirectional
A.5.16↔A.5.18 pair (identity disable + credential revocation) is
a common finding when only one side fires.', 55, 'access_rights_lifecycle.yaml', '2026-08-02 12:49:55.371995+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('business_continuity', 'Business Continuity & ICT Readiness', 'Continuity of information security during disruption (A.5.29) +
ICT readiness for business continuity (A.5.30) + backup /
restore verification (A.8.13). The BCM lane in the ISMS.', 'ISO27001:2022', 'Continuity claims are only credible when tested. Auditors look
for: (1) the plan itself; (2) evidence of at least annual
activation tests with named participants + outcomes; (3) recent
restore-test records (A.8.13) — not just "we take backups";
(4) integration with the incident-response bundle so activation
triggers are documented.', 50, 'business_continuity.yaml', '2026-08-02 12:49:55.371995+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('change_management', 'Change Management', 'ISMS change control (clause 6.3) + operational change management
(A.8.32) — the discipline that prevents the "we made a security-
relevant change and never assessed impact" finding.', 'ISO27001:2022', 'Auditors trace a sample of changes end-to-end: (1) the change
request (CRF); (2) the impact assessment referencing risk +
affected controls; (3) approval by an authorised party; (4)
post-implementation review + any rollback / incident linkage.', 65, 'change_management.yaml', '2026-08-02 12:49:55.371995+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('continual_improvement', 'Continual Improvement (NC, Audit, Management Review)', 'The management-system feedback loop mandated by ISO 27001 clauses
9.1 (monitoring/measurement), 9.2 (internal audit), 9.3 (management
review), 10.1 (continual improvement), 10.2 (nonconformity +
corrective action). The clauses that make the ISMS a system, not
a one-off certification.', 'ISO27001:2022', 'Auditors trace the loop: monitoring finds something (9.1) or an
event exposes it (10.2), corrective action is planned + tracked
(10.2 register), internal audit verifies the fix (9.2), and
management review takes the summary + drives strategic
improvement (9.3 → 10.1). Broken loops are the most common
finding on Stage-2 audits.', 70, 'continual_improvement.yaml', '2026-08-02 12:49:55.371995+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('employee_lifecycle', 'Employee Lifecycle (Onboarding to Offboarding)', 'Human resources security across the employment cycle covering
ISO 27001 Annex A.6 (people controls) + access + asset lifecycle
(A.5.11 return, A.5.16-18 identity + access rights). Load-bearing
bundle — HR events drive most identity + access change traffic.', 'multi', 'Auditors sample joiners and leavers and trace: joiner — screening
(A.6.1) + signed terms (A.6.2) + NDA (A.6.6) + awareness training
(A.6.3) + identity provisioning (A.5.16-17); leaver — return of
assets (A.5.11) + revocation (A.5.16-18) + post-employment
responsibilities (A.6.5) + disciplinary evidence if applicable
(A.6.4).', 45, 'employee_lifecycle.yaml', '2026-08-02 12:49:55.371995+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('incident_response', 'Security Incident Response Lifecycle', 'End-to-end incident handling from event detection through
post-incident review. Covers ISO 27001 A.5.24-27 (preparation,
event assessment, response, post-incident) + GDPR Art.33-34
(regulatory notification) + A.6.8 (event reporting culture).', 'multi', 'Auditors want to see this bundle exercised in real conditions.
Common findings on immature ISMS: (1) no formal event-triage
criteria (A.5.25); (2) incident register incomplete or missing
closure notes (A.5.26); (3) no evidence of framework drills
(A.5.24); (4) breach-notification decision workflow absent
(Art.33). A working bundle demonstrates the incident capability
under scrutiny.', 20, 'incident_response.yaml', '2026-08-02 12:49:55.371995+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('risk_assessment_treatment', 'Risk Assessment & Treatment Cycle', 'The full risk lifecycle mandated by ISO 27001 clauses 6.1.2
(risk assessment methodology), 6.1.3 (risk treatment + SoA),
8.2 (operational risk assessment), and 8.3 (risk treatment
execution). The most-audited clause set in ISO 27001.', 'ISO27001:2022', 'Auditors first ask for the documented methodology (6.1.2), then
the risk register showing it''s been applied, then the SoA
(6.1.3) with justifications, then the risk treatment plan with
ownership + due dates, then evidence of operational execution
(8.2 + 8.3). Missing SoA justification-per-control is the #1
finding on Stage-1 audits.', 30, 'risk_assessment_treatment.yaml', '2026-08-02 12:49:55.371995+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('breach_notification', 'Personal Data Breach Notification', 'The 72-hour supervisory-authority notification workflow under
GDPR Art.33 and the affected-subject notification under Art.34.
Distinct from the general incident-response bundle — this is the
regulatory-clock lane that triggers only when personal data is
involved.', 'GDPR:2016/679', 'Supervisory authority questions on a breach investigation: (1)
when did you become aware; (2) show me the risk-assessment
decision (why notification was / was not required); (3) show me
the notification letter and receipt; (4) show me the internal
breach register including breaches you did NOT notify (Art.33.5
requires all breaches on record).', 25, 'breach_notification.yaml', '2026-08-02 12:57:08.234677+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('consent_and_lawful_basis', 'Consent & Lawful Basis Lifecycle', 'How your organisation establishes and documents the lawful basis
for every processing operation — and, where consent is the basis,
how consent is obtained, refreshed, and withdrawn. Covers GDPR
Art.6-9 (lawfulness + special categories) via the ISO 27701
A.7.2 controller controls.', 'ISO27701:2019', 'A supervisory authority looks for: (1) the mapping of every
processing operation to a specific Art.6 lawful basis, with
Art.9 conditions where special categories are processed; (2)
documented consent capture records where consent is the basis
(Art.7(1) burden of proof rests on the controller); (3) a
working withdrawal mechanism (Art.7(3)); (4) evidence that
special-category processing has documented Art.9(2) exceptions.', 15, 'consent_and_lawful_basis.yaml', '2026-08-02 13:09:50.852259+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('data_transfers_disclosures', 'Cross-Border Transfers & Third-Party Disclosures', 'Cross-jurisdiction PII movement and disclosures to third parties.
Distinct from RoPA (which is inventory) and supplier onboarding
(which is procurement). This bundle handles the ongoing operational
discipline of Art.44-49 transfers + third-party disclosure logging,
from both the controller (A.7.5) and processor (B.8.5) angles.', 'ISO27701:2019', 'Post-Schrems II, transfers are the most-scrutinized area of GDPR
compliance. Auditors expect: (1) a transfer register mapping each
transfer to its Art.46 mechanism (SCCs, BCRs, adequacy decision,
derogation); (2) evidence of Transfer Impact Assessments per
EDPB Recommendations 01/2020; (3) records of legally-binding
disclosure requests (B.8.5.5) with the decision + timeline; (4)
clean processor / subprocessor engagement records (B.8.5.6-8).', 42, 'data_transfers_disclosures.yaml', '2026-08-02 13:09:50.852259+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('dpia_workflow', 'Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA)', 'The DPIA workflow for high-risk processing operations under
GDPR Art.35 — screening (is a DPIA needed?), assessment
(structured analysis per Art.35(7)), consultation (DPO +
potentially SA under Art.36), and remediation (residual risk
acceptance or process redesign).', 'GDPR:2016/679', 'A supervisory authority reviewing DPIA maturity looks for: (1)
a documented screening trigger (systematic monitoring, large-
scale special categories, public spaces, etc.); (2) a formal
methodology aligned with WP29 wp248 rev.01; (3) evidence the
DPO was consulted (Art.35(2)); (4) the register of DPIAs
performed with linkage to the operations they cover.', 35, 'dpia_workflow.yaml', '2026-08-02 13:09:50.852259+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('dsr_management', 'Subject Access & Data Subject Rights', 'How your organisation receives, verifies, and responds to data
subject requests under GDPR Chapter III — access (Art.15),
rectification (Art.16), and erasure (Art.17). A DPO should be
able to answer a customer''s "how do you handle my rights request?"
with this bundle alone.', 'GDPR:2016/679', 'A supervisory authority spot-check asks: (1) show me the DSR
policy signed by top management; (2) show me the procedure staff
follow; (3) show me last month''s DSR register with response
timelines; (4) show me one closed DSR file with the response
letter and identity-verification record. This bundle covers all
four. Missing any one is a common Art.15 finding.', 10, 'dsr_management.yaml', '2026-08-02 13:09:50.852259+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('pii_lifecycle', 'PII Lifecycle (Privacy Engineering)', 'The proactive privacy discipline — data minimization, retention,
deletion, temporary-file handling, and secure transmission
across the PII lifecycle. Distinct from DSR (which is reactive)
and from consent (which is upstream). Covers GDPR Art.5.1(c-e)
storage limitation + data minimization via ISO 27701 A.7.4
controller controls.', 'ISO27701:2019', 'This is the "systems + engineering" side of privacy. Auditors
look for: (1) documented retention schedules per data category
with enforcement mechanisms; (2) evidence of active minimization
(fields collected only when needed); (3) end-of-processing
deletion records — not just "policy says we delete"; (4)
temp-file discipline (A.7.4.6) — often absent on immature ISMS.', 47, 'pii_lifecycle.yaml', '2026-08-02 13:09:50.852259+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('privacy_notice_transparency', 'Privacy Notice & Transparency', 'How your organisation informs data subjects about processing —
the privacy notice at collection (Art.13/14), the transparency
discipline (Art.12), and the ability to modify or withdraw
choices (Art.7(3), A.7.3.4). Distinct from DSR (which is reactive
request handling) — this is the proactive information layer.', 'ISO27701:2019', 'Regulators check: (1) the current privacy notice exists and is
accessible at the point of data collection; (2) it contains all
Art.13 or Art.14 mandatory items (identity, purposes, basis,
recipients, retention, rights, source, transfers); (3) notice
distribution is evidenced — showing the notice existed at time
of collection, not retrofit; (4) the withdrawal / preference-
modification mechanism works and is documented.', 12, 'privacy_notice_transparency.yaml', '2026-08-02 13:09:50.852259+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('processor_operations', 'Processor Operations (Serving Customer Controllers)', 'When the tenant IS a processor (typical for cloud SaaS), this
bundle covers the operational discipline of serving customer
controllers — customer agreements, purpose adherence, infringing
instruction handling, and support for the customer''s own data
subject request obligations. Distinct from `supplier_onboarding`
(which is the tenant as controller buying services).', 'ISO27701:2019', 'A customer controller auditing their processor asks: (1) show
me our customer agreement + the mandatory Art.28(3) content;
(2) show me how you refuse or flag an infringing instruction
(B.8.2.4 — auditor tests processor''s judgment); (3) show me
your processor RoPA (Art.30(2)); (4) show me evidence you
support our DSR obligations (B.8.3.1); (5) show me the end-of-
service data return / disposal path (B.8.4.2).', 43, 'processor_operations.yaml', '2026-08-02 13:09:50.852259+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('records_of_processing', 'Records of Processing Activities (RoPA)', 'GDPR Art.30 records of processing — the always-current inventory
of processing operations that a supervisory authority can demand
on 24-hour notice. Requires the controller-facing register (30.1)
and, for processors, the processor-facing register (30.2).', 'GDPR:2016/679', 'The RoPA is the FIRST document a supervisory authority requests
in almost any investigation. Auditors check: (1) it exists in
writing (30.4); (2) it covers all Art.30(1) mandatory fields per
processing activity; (3) it''s kept current (annual review at
minimum); (4) data-flow mapping is coherent — where PII actually
lives ties to entries in the RoPA.', 60, 'records_of_processing.yaml', '2026-08-02 13:09:50.852259+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topics (slug, title, description, primary_framework, auditor_expects, display_order, source_file, last_loaded_at, last_loaded_by) VALUES ('supplier_onboarding', 'Supplier / Processor Onboarding', 'End-to-end vendor onboarding covering ISO 27001 A.5.19-A.5.23
(supplier security requirements + agreements + supply-chain
management) + GDPR Art.28 (processor obligations). The bundle
a procurement team runs on every new vendor.', 'multi', 'Auditors sample suppliers and ask: show me the agreement (A.5.20
+ Art.28), show me the risk assessment (A.5.19), show me the
ongoing review record (A.5.22), and — for processors — show me
the DPA (Art.28) with the Art.28(3) mandatory clauses.', 40, 'supplier_onboarding.yaml', '2026-08-02 13:09:50.852259+00', 'topics/load_to_postgres.py');
INSERT INTO public.topic_leaves (topic_slug, leaf_id, role, workflow_order, role_note) VALUES ('access_rights_lifecycle', 'req:A.5.15:access_control_policy', 'primary_policy', 1, 'The over-arching access-control policy — sets principles
(least privilege, need-to-know, segregation) that A.5.16-18
operational procedures implement.');
INSERT INTO public.topic_leaves (topic_slug, leaf_id, role, workflow_order, role_note) VALUES ('access_rights_lifecycle', 'req:A.5.15:management_approval', 'evidence', 2, NULL);
INSERT INTO public.topic_leaves (topic_slug, leaf_id, role, workflow_order, role_note) VALUES ('access_rights_lifecycle', 'req:A.5.16:identity_management_procedure', 'primary_procedure', 3, NULL);
INSERT INTO public.topic_leaves (topic_slug, leaf_id, role, workflow_order, role_note) VALUES ('access_rights_lifecycle', 'req:A.5.16:identity_register', 'primary_register', 4, NULL);
INSERT INTO public.topic_leaves (topic_slug, leaf_id, role, workflow_order, role_note) VALUES ('access_rights_lifecycle', 'req:A.5.16:identity_revocation_record', 'evidence', 5, NULL);
INSERT INTO public.topic_leaves (topic_slug, leaf_id, role, workflow_order, role_note) VALUES ('access_rights_lifecycle', 'req:A.5.17:authentication_information_procedure', 'primary_procedure', 6, NULL);
